    <desc>Choose what the <key>Backspace</key> and <key>Delete</key> keys
    send to programs.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Backspace and Delete keys</title>

  <p>A program in a terminal does not see keys, but the characters or
  <link xref="overview">escape sequences</link> that the terminal sends for
  them. Traditionally, <key>Backspace</key> deletes the character before the
  cursor and <key>Delete</key> the one after it, but programs and systems
  differ in what they expect for these keys. If one of them does something
  else in a program, for example prints <sys>^H</sys> or <sys>~</sys>, you can
  change what it sends:</p>

  <steps>
    <item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and
    select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
    <item><p>In the <gui>Profiles</gui> group, click the profile you want to
    change.</p></item>
    <item><p>In the <gui>Compatibility</gui> group, set <gui>Backspace Key</gui>
    and <gui>Delete Key</gui>.</p></item>
  </steps>

  <p>Both offer the same choices:</p>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Automatic</gui></title>
      <p>The usual choice: <key>Backspace</key> sends the erase character of
      the terminal, as <cmd>stty</cmd> sets it, and <key>Delete</key> sends
      the escape sequence <sys>ESC [ 3 ~</sys>.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Control-H</gui></title>
      <p>Sends the backspace character, <sys>^H</sys>, which some older
      programs and systems expect.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>ASCII DEL</gui></title>
      <p>Sends the delete character, <sys>^?</sys>, which most systems today
      use for <key>Backspace</key>.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Escape Sequence</gui></title>
      <p>Sends <sys>ESC [ 3 ~</sys>, which programs understand as
      <key>Delete</key>.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>TTY Erase</gui></title>
      <p>Sends the erase character of the terminal, as <cmd>stty</cmd> sets
      it.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <note style="tip">
    <p>Make a <link xref="pref-profiles#new-profile">profile</link> with other
    settings for the computers or programs that need them, and keep your usual
    one as it is.</p>
  </note>

  <p>The same group also sets the <link xref="pref-profile-encoding">character
  encoding</link> and the <link xref="pref-profile-char-width">width of
  ambiguous characters</link>.</p>
